# M3 global policy patch

Target: `~/.codex/AGENTS.md`, preserving the shared global-file link.

Format: focused before/after prose patch applied after M1 and M2. Each BEFORE block identifies
either unchanged original local text or the exact staged text from an earlier patch. No whole-file
replacement is required.

Activation: R3 must be a recorded full merged commit containing all M3 acceptance requirements.
Install R3, or a descendant retaining earlier support, before applying this patch.

Source: approved artifact sections 04, 05.2, 05.4, 07, and 08.

## 1. Name the integrated planning exception

BEFORE:
| Planning, reasoning, architecture | Fable, high effort |

AFTER:
| Standalone planning and required independent design review | Fable, high effort |
| Integrated planning and implementation in one Dux deliverable | Opus, max effort |

## 2. Activate integrated Opus ownership

BEFORE:
**Implementation model.** Retain Sonnet medium for bounded implementation and Opus max for complex
implementation. Standalone planning and required independent design reviews remain Fable high.
Integrated plan-and-build continuation is unavailable until Dux R3 is installed.

AFTER:
**Implementation model.** Retain Sonnet medium for bounded implementation and Opus max for complex
implementation. For an integrated plan-and-build task, Opus owns both phases. This avoids an
unverified live model switch and nested implementation. Standalone planning and required
independent design reviews remain Fable high.

## 3. Align the planning owner

BEFORE:
1. **Fable plans in full before any code**: target files, constraints, risks, acceptance criteria,
   open questions. No design decisions left for mid-implementation.

AFTER:
1. **Plan in full before implementation**: target files, constraints, risks, acceptance criteria,
   open questions. No design decisions left for mid-implementation. Standalone planning uses
   Fable high. For integrated Dux plan-and-build work, the owning Opus worker writes the plan and
   pauses for recorded approval before implementation.

## 4. Preserve approval only for the permitted progress edits

BEFORE:
4. **After approval, stop planning.** No per-task plans, no re-planning between milestones. If
   reality diverges enough to matter, say so in one line and adjust. **Amending the approved plan
   mid-implementation is re-planning.** When a task genuinely has to be added, add it and record it
   in the plan header as a divergence, with one line on what changed and why. Three amendments in
   one milestone means the plan was approved too early: stop and say so rather than adding a fourth.

AFTER:
4. **After approval, stop planning.** No per-task plans or repeated planning between milestones.
   For integrated Dux work, approval records the exact committed plan path, approved commit, and
   task range. Only checkbox state changes on existing task entries and progress-only updates
   inside the designated three-line "where this stands" block preserve approval.
   Checkbox changes must not change task text, identity, order, or membership.
   All other plan changes invalidate approval, including changed acceptance criteria, task
   descriptions, constraints, membership, and substantive amendments. A changed approved task
   range also requires renewed approval, even when the plan file is unchanged.
   Progress text cannot authorize new work. A substantive amendment recorded in the status block
   still requires a substantive plan change and renewed approval before affected work proceeds.
   Record a required divergence in the plan header. Three amendments in one milestone mean the
   plan was approved too early: stop rather than adding a fourth.

## 5. Keep integrated ownership through the handoff

BEFORE:
5. Hand the implementer the plan, target files, constraints, and acceptance criteria. Not the
   exploratory discussion.

AFTER:
5. For a separate implementation assignment, hand the implementer the plan, target files,
   constraints, and acceptance criteria, not the exploratory discussion. Integrated Dux
   plan-and-build work remains with its owning Opus worker after approval is recorded.

## 6. Activate same-session answers and approval

BEFORE:
- **Until R3 is installed, questions and approval use existing recovery.** Integrated
  plan-and-build continuation and recorded prerequisite dispatch remain unavailable.

AFTER:
- **Questions and recoverable blockers within the deliverable continue the live owning worker.**
  Extend PR #46's existing round mechanism with explicit purposes: feedback, answer, and approval.
  An answer requires the matching waiting state and a live, positively parked owner.
  Approval additionally identifies the committed plan path, task range, and approved commit.
  An ordinary answer cannot authorize implementation.
- **Every resumed stretch receives fresh run identity and supervision.** Failed or dead sessions
  use the existing explicit recovery path. Retain the eight-round limit across continuations;
  reaching it requires a clearer task boundary, not an automatic context reset.
- **Integrated work retains the `ship` task shape with `planning|implementation` phase metadata.**
  A planning-phase ship task may start without an existing plan only when explicitly declared as
  such. It creates the plan and pauses at `needs-decision`. Approval records the committed plan
  and approved task range before advancing to implementation.
- **Completion proof requires valid approval and completed approved tasks.** Compare the current
  plan with the approved commit, permitting only the specified checkbox and designated status-block
  progress edits, then check the current completion boxes for the approved range. Checkbox updates
  preserve authorization; they do not replace delivery evidence.
- **Use one implementation worktree from the start.** Never promote a plan-only task's worktree or
  change its permissions midway. An explicit plan-only assignment remains a Fable planning
  worker whose deliverable is the plan.
- **Dependent repository dispatch is available through the verified prerequisite rule below.**
  Activation still passes the global one-active-worker admission check.

